WEBVTT
TITLE Graphs: Matching and Colouring — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Two questions, one graph. A matching picks edges that share no endpoint. A colouring splits the vertices into groups with no edge inside a group.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Here the three thick edges are a matching of size 3. No two of them meet, so no vertex is used twice.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
On the same graph, three colours suffice: each colour class is an independent set, and every edge crosses between two classes.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Both numbers are computed from the vertex set and the edge set. Move the vertices and nothing changes; the picture is not the object.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
A matching M is a set of edges with no shared endpoint. A vertex is saturated by M if an edge of M touches it, and free otherwise.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Maximal means no further edge can be added. Maximum means no matching on this graph is larger. The first is about this set, the second about the whole graph.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The two thick edges are maximal: every other edge touches a saturated vertex. Yet three edges fit, so they are not maximum.

8
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:00.000
A perfect matching saturates every vertex, so it exists only when the vertex count is even. Maximum matching size is written with the Greek letter nu.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
An alternating path starts at a free vertex, alternates between edges not in the matching and edges in it, and ends at a free vertex.

10
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Such a path is called augmenting. Swap every edge on it: drop the matching edges, take the others. The matching grows by exactly one.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Berge's theorem: a matching is maximum exactly when no augmenting path exists. So the test for maximum is a search, not a comparison.

12
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Repeating the search gives an algorithm: start with any matching, augment until no path is left. Each round adds one edge, so it stops after at most n/2 rounds.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Take a bipartite graph with the left side L and the right side R. Write N(S) for the set of neighbours of a subset S of L.

14
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Here three left vertices share only two neighbours. A matching cannot saturate all three, because each of them needs a different partner.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Hall's condition: for every subset S of L, the size of N(S) is at least the size of S. Hall's theorem: that condition is also sufficient.

16
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Add one edge and the smallest short subset disappears. Now every subset has enough neighbours, and a matching saturating L exists.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
A vertex cover is a set of vertices touching every edge. Here four vertices cover all the edges of this bipartite graph.

18
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.000
A matching and a cover never share more than one vertex per matching edge, so the matching is never larger than the cover.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Konig's theorem: in a bipartite graph the two numbers are equal. The largest matching and the smallest cover have the same size.

20
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:30.000
On a triangle the equality fails: the largest matching has one edge and the smallest cover needs two vertices. The odd cycle is what breaks it.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
The chromatic number, written chi, is the fewest colours in a proper colouring. A complete graph on n vertices needs n colours: every pair is joined.

22
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:45.000
A cycle needs two colours when its length is even and three when it is odd. Walk the colours round: an odd number of steps returns you to the start with the wrong colour.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.000
A tree needs two, and so does every bipartite graph, because bipartite is exactly the same as two-colourable. Petersen needs three.

24
00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The clique number is a lower bound on chi, never an equality. The Mycielskian of a five-cycle has no triangle at all and still needs four colours.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
First-fit greedy takes the vertices in some order and gives each the smallest colour no neighbour already has.

26
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:15.000
At most delta plus one colours: when a vertex is coloured, at most delta neighbours are already coloured, so among delta plus one colours one is always free.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.000
The order matters. This bipartite graph is two-colourable, yet one order makes greedy use three colours.

28
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:30.000
So greedy gives an upper bound and the clique gives a lower bound. When the two meet, the chromatic number is settled without any search.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Brooks' theorem: a connected graph needs at most delta colours, unless it is complete or an odd cycle.

30
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.000
The exceptions are real: a complete graph needs delta plus one, and an odd cycle needs three against a delta of two.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.000
The gap can be large. A star has maximum degree n minus one and needs only two colours, so chi sits far below delta.

32
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Useful bounds together: the clique number is at most chi, which is at most delta plus one, and at most delta once Brooks applies.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Edge colouring colours the edges so that edges meeting at a vertex get different colours. The fewest colours is the chromatic index, chi prime.

34
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Every colour class is a matching, so the edges at one vertex need distinct colours: the index is at least delta. On a bipartite graph it is exactly delta.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Vizing's theorem: for every simple graph the index is delta or delta plus one. So an edge-colouring question has only two candidate answers.

36
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:30.000
A triangle has delta two and index three, so it is class two. The cube graph is bipartite with delta three, so its index is three while its chromatic number is two.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.000
A graph is planar if it can be drawn in the plane with no two edges crossing. For a connected planar drawing, n minus m plus f equals two.

38
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Every face has at least three edges, and every edge borders two faces, so m is at most 3n minus 6. In a bipartite planar graph the bound tightens to 2n minus 4.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.000
K5 has ten edges where nine is the limit, and K3,3 has nine where eight is the limit. Both are non-planar, and Kuratowski says every non-planar graph contains one of them.

40
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Every planar graph is four-colourable, and three colours do not always suffice: an odd cycle drawn flat already needs three, and there are planar graphs needing four.

41
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.000
A fixed procedure: name the object, check bipartiteness, find a bound from each side, then look for a certificate.

42
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Trap one: answering maximal when the question said maximum, or quoting Konig on a graph that is not bipartite.

43
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Trap two: treating the clique number as the chromatic number, or greedy as exact. One is a bound below, the other a bound above.

44
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Trap three: confusing vertex colours with edge colours, and forgetting that Vizing allows two answers. Delta or delta plus one, decided by a construction.

